## 6. Input and Representation Constraints

- Input modality and shape: "It first splits an input RGB image into non-overlapping patches by a patch splitting module, like ViT." (Section 3.1)
- Fixed patch size and tokenization: "In our implementation, we use a patch size of 4x4 and thus the feature dimension of each patch is 4x4x3=48." (Section 3.1)
- Token count tied to image resolution: "The Transformer blocks maintain the number of tokens (H/4 x W/4)." (Section 3.1)
- Hierarchical downsampling by patch merging: "The first patch merging layer concatenates the features of each group of 2x2 neighboring patches... This reduces the number of tokens by a multiple of 2x2=4 (2x downsampling of resolution)." (Section 3.1)
- Fixed window size for attention: "Supposing each window contains M x M patches... the latter is linear when M is fixed (set to 7 by default)." (Section 3.2)
- Padding requirement for window size divisibility: "To make the window size (M,M) divisible by the feature map size of (h,w), bottom-right padding is employed on the feature map if needed." (Section 4.1)
- Input resizing in detection: "multi-scale training [8, 52] (resizing the input such that the shorter side is between 480 and 800 while the longer side is at most 1333)" (Section 4.2)

## 7. Context Window and Attention Structure

- Maximum sequence length: Per attention window is M^2 patches, with M fixed by default. Evidence: "Supposing each window contains M x M patches... the latter is linear when M is fixed (set to 7 by default)." (Section 3.2)
- Fixed vs variable sequence length: Per-window length is fixed by M, while total token count varies with image size. Evidence: "The Transformer blocks maintain the number of tokens (H/4 x W/4)." (Section 3.1) "Supposing each window contains M x M patches..." (Section 3.2)
- Attention type: Windowed and hierarchical with shifted windows. Evidence: "we propose a hierarchical Transformer whose representation is computed with Shifted windows." (Abstract) "For efficient modeling, we propose to compute self-attention within local windows." (Section 3.2) "To introduce cross-window connections... we propose a shifted window partitioning approach" (Section 3.2).
- Mechanisms to manage computational cost: windowed attention, shifted windows, patch merging, cyclic shift with masking. Evidence: "The shifted windowing scheme brings greater efficiency by limiting self-attention computation to non-overlapping local windows while also allowing for cross-window connection." (Abstract) "This reduces the number of tokens by a multiple of 2x2=4 (2x downsampling of resolution)." (Section 3.1) "Here, we propose a more efficient batch computation approach by cyclic-shifting... so a masking mechanism is employed" (Section 3.2).

## 8. Positional Encoding (Critical Section)

- Mechanism: Relative position bias (bias-based). Evidence: "including a relative position bias B in R^{M^2 x M^2} to each head in computing similarity" (Section 3.2) and "Attention(Q, K, V) = SoftMax(QK^{T}/sqrt{d} + B)V" (Section 3.2).
- Where applied: Attention bias term inside the attention similarity computation. Evidence: "SoftMax(QK^{T}/sqrt{d} + B)V" (Section 3.2).
- Fixed vs modified/ablated: Compared against no position encoding and absolute position embedding, with relative bias used in the final implementation. Evidence: "We observe significant improvements over counterparts without this bias term or that use absolute position embedding, as shown in Table 4. Further adding absolute position embedding to the input as in [19] drops performance slightly, thus it is not adopted in our implementation." (Section 3.2) "Table 4 shows comparisons of different position embedding approaches." (Section 4.4).

## 9. Positional Encoding as a Variable

- Role: Treated as an ablated design element rather than a fixed, unexamined assumption. Evidence: "Table 4 shows comparisons of different position embedding approaches." (Section 4.4) "Swin-T with relative position bias yields +1.2%/+0.8% top-1 accuracy... in relation to those without position encoding and with absolute position embedding, respectively" (Section 4.4).
- Multiple encodings compared: Yes (no position encoding, absolute position embedding, relative position bias, abs+rel). Evidence: Table 4 rows: "no pos.", "abs. pos.", "abs.+rel. pos.", "rel. pos." (Table 4).
- Claim about criticality: They report relative bias improves results and absolute position embedding can hurt; no claim that PE is unimportant. Evidence: "Further adding absolute position embedding to the input as in [19] drops performance slightly, thus it is not adopted in our implementation." (Section 3.2).

